a=ex.groupby('modality')[['auroc','auprc','raw_brier','brier','raw_ece','ece']].mean();T8=table('Expert performance on available records, raw $\\rightarrow$ separately recalibrated Brier/ECE. Cohorts differ by availability.','tab:expert',['Expert','AUROC','AUPRC','Brier (raw/cal)','ECE (raw/cal)'],[[k,val(z.auroc),val(z.auprc),val(z.raw_brier,3)+'/'+val(z.brier,3),val(z.raw_ece,3)+'/'+val(z.ece,3)] for k,z in zip(['Clinical','Laboratory','Imaging'],a.itertuples())],'lrrrr')
orig=Path('AURA.tex').read_text();tex=orig
tex=tex.replace('AURA: Evaluating Disagreement-Aware Selective Multimodal Learning for Axial Spondyloarthritis in a Synthetic Benchmark','AURA: Assessing the Limits of Disagreement-Aware Selective Prediction in an Axial Spondyloarthritis-Inspired Synthetic Benchmark')
a=tex.index('\\begin{abstract}');b=tex.index('\\section{Introduction}')
tex=tex[:a]+r'''\begin{abstract}
Inter-modality disagreement is often proposed as an uncertainty signal for selective prediction, but its incremental value beyond confidence and evidence quality is not guaranteed. We investigate this question using AURA, a lightweight reliability-weighted fusion test bed, in an axial spondyloarthritis (axSpA)-inspired synthetic benchmark. Ten independent simulation seeds, seven observation or population conditions and six classifiers are evaluated under matched ranking protocols; additional native AURA ablations and nine sensitivity settings isolate the role of disagreement. Relative to equal fusion, learned fusion improves AUROC by 0.00251 (95\% paired seed-level interval [0.00200, 0.00302]) and Brier score by $-0.00150$ ([$-0.00178,-0.00122$]). A native learned ranker reduces area under the risk-coverage curve (AURC) relative to entropy by $-0.00424$ ([$-0.00568,-0.00280$]), but adding disagreement yields $-0.00014$ ([$-0.00069,0.00042$]); all nine sensitivity intervals span zero. Early logistic regression retains the lowest mean AURC under shared ranking protocols. These results constrain the methodological claim that disagreement is independently useful: neither its benefit nor its absence in other settings is established. All data are simulated, with illustrative rather than clinically calibrated parameters. The study evaluates algorithmic behavior in this generator, not axSpA diagnostic performance, clinical evidence relationships or readiness.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
selective prediction, multimodal fusion, missing modalities, simulation, uncertainty ranking, attribution stability, negative results
\end{IEEEkeywords}
'''+tex[b:]
start=tex.index('This work proposes AURA');end=tex.index('\\section{Related Work',start)
tex=tex[:start]+r'''This study uses AURA as an auditable test bed rather than claiming a novel state-of-the-art architecture. Its contribution is an investigation of the limits of a methodological claim: (i) a reproducible axSpA-inspired synthetic stress benchmark; (ii) matched classifier and ranking comparisons that separate the effects of fusion from the incremental effect of dispersion; (iii) paired uncertainty intervals, sensitivity analyses and held-out permutation diagnostics; and (iv) algebraically checked and cross-seed tested model attributions. A negative result is informative precisely because a plausible mechanism need not improve selective prediction. The simulated binary class is not an independently adjudicated clinical diagnosis.
'''+tex[end:]
tex=tex.replace('\\section{Proposed AURA Method}','\\section{AURA Test Bed and Ranking Protocols}')
tex=tex.replace('Lower AURC means better selective performance, conditional on the underlying classifier.',r'''Specifically, $\mathrm{AURC}=n^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^{n}j^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{j}e_{(i)}$, where parentheses denote increasing error-score order. Exact ties use the expected cumulative error under uniformly random within-tie ordering: each tied observation contributes the mean error of its tie block. Lower AURC means better selective performance, conditional on the underlying classifier. Fixed-coverage risk $R_{80}$ is the cumulative risk at $\lfloor0.8n\rfloor$ retained observations (same tie convention). It is distinct from risk at a fixed score cutoff.''')
tex=tex.replace('ridge regression estimates the squared probability error $\\ell_k=(p_k-Y)^2$',r'ridge regression estimates the squared probability error $\ell_k=\bigl(p_k-Y\bigr)^2$')
tex=tex.replace('The full ranker adds $4D$ to $t_0$.',r'''The native full ranker adds $4D$ to $t_0$. For fair cross-classifier comparisons, we additionally define a shared-context protocol: the weighted expert variance and $4D$ are computed from the same auxiliary equal-weight expert ensemble for all six classifiers, while entropy is computed from each classifier's own calibrated probability. Availability, quality and mask indicators are identical across classifiers. Each classifier has its own logistic error ranker, fitted on its own error labels on the same development records, with the same standardized features and regularization ($C=1$). Thus all classifiers have access to the same ranking information, including the extra cost of fitting the auxiliary experts. This is a matched training protocol, not a claim that ranker coefficients or predictions are identical across classifiers. Native AURA uses its learned fusion weights for variance and disagreement; its matched ablation remains the primary RQ3 comparison.''')
tex=tex.replace('HLA-B27 & B & -2.2','HLA-B27 & B & $-2.2$')
tex=tex.replace('Quality & Quality changes to $\\mathrm{Beta}(2,4)$','Quality & $q_k\\sim\\mathrm{Beta}(2,4)$; more observation noise')
tex=tex.replace('For every condition, at least one modality is retained; nominal dropout rates therefore differ from realized missingness.',r'''For every condition, at least one modality is retained; nominal dropout rates therefore differ from realized missingness. In the Quality and Combined conditions, drawing lower $q_k$ also increases the observation corruption via the same noise equations, rather than merely changing a metadata input. Repair of all-zero masks is a simulator design choice that conditions the evaluated problem on nonempty evidence, not a realistic acquisition or deferral policy; no inference is made about entirely missing records.''')
a=tex.index('Eight configurations are evaluated:');b=tex.index('\\section{Results}',a)
tex=tex[:a]+r'''Six classifiers are evaluated: mask-aware early logistic regression, random forest (80 trees, minimum leaf size 8), histogram gradient boosting (100 iterations, at most 15 leaves), equal late fusion, quality late fusion, and learned AURA fusion. Early fusion concatenates zero-filled features, mask indicators and masked quality. All six receive dedicated probability recalibration. Each is evaluated with entropy, a shared-context ranker without $D$, and a shared-context ranker with $D$; AURA additionally receives two native rankers (20 configurations, 1,400 seed-condition-configuration evaluations). The same expert predictions, acquired observations and partitions are retained from the initial benchmark. No hyperparameter selection is made using test data.

We calculate AUROC, area under the precision-recall curve as average precision (AUPRC), Brier score, log loss, accuracy, ten-bin equal-width expected calibration error (ECE), AURC, fixed-coverage selective risk, and error-detection AUROC. Macro means weight each condition equally, then each seed equally. Paired contrasts first average the seven condition-level differences within each seed, then use a two-sided Student-$t$ interval with nine degrees of freedom across ten seed means. These intervals reflect variability under the stipulated simulator, not clinical uncertainty. Secondary comparisons are exploratory and unadjusted for multiplicity. Fixed thresholds are estimated only on the ranker-development partition; ties at the threshold are retained.

Sensitivity analyses vary one factor at a time around the default: bootstrap members $B\in\{3,5,10\}$, lower gate clipping $\in\{0.01,0.03,0.10\}$, ridge $\alpha\in\{1,5,20\}$, and ranker inverse regularization $C\in\{0.1,1,10\}$. The nine configurations reuse identical observations and partitions and nested bootstrap members; all gate, calibration and ranker fits are recomputed as appropriate. For the default native ranker we report standardized $4D$ coefficients and odds ratios per development standard deviation. In each held-out condition, $4D$ is shuffled ten times with fixed ranker and class probabilities; we report increases in AURC and decreases in error AUROC. Marginal permutation can disrupt feature dependencies, so this is a diagnostic, not a causal or conditional importance estimate. Individual experts are separately recalibrated on available calibration records and evaluated on available test records. For attribution stability, 150 independent complete-condition anchor records (fixed across seeds) are passed through all ten fitted default systems. All 45 seed pairs are compared for absolute attribution difference, sign agreement and agreement on the largest absolute attribution. The pairs are dependent; their means are descriptive, not 45 independent samples.
'''+tex[b:]
a=tex.index('\\section{Results}');b=tex.index('\\section{Discussion and Limitations}',a)
tex=tex[:a]+r'''\section{Results}
\subsection{RQ1: Fusion performance}
'''+T1+T3+r'''Table~\ref{tab:macro} reports probability metrics independently of ranking. Learned fusion yields higher AUROC and AUPRC and lower Brier and ECE than equal and quality weighting; the paired seed-level intervals in Table~\ref{tab:fusionci} exclude zero for all eight contrasts, although gains are small and intervals are not multiplicity-adjusted. Early logistic regression has the highest mean AUROC and AUPRC and lowest Brier; AURA minus early logistic AUROC is $-0.00254$ (95\% interval [$-0.00427,-0.00081$]), AUPRC $-0.01372$ ([$-0.01736,-0.01009$]), and Brier $+0.00199$ ([0.00087, 0.00310]). ECE difference $+0.00173$ ([$-0.00128,0.00475$]) is inconclusive. These are simulator-specific effects, not medical performance estimates.
\subsection{RQ2: Matched ranking comparisons}
'''+T2+T4+r'''Under common ranking feature definitions (Table~\ref{tab:rank}), AURA outperforms equal and quality fusion in mean AURC, both with and without $D$. With the full shared ranker, paired AURA-minus-equal and AURA-minus-quality differences are $-0.00162$ ([$-0.00195,-0.00129$]) and $-0.00176$ ([$-0.00271,-0.00081$]). Early logistic has the lowest mean AURC in each column. Under the full shared protocol, AURA minus early logistic is $+0.00193$ ([$-0.00073,0.00459$]); neither superiority nor equivalence is established. Under entropy alone this contrast is $+0.00664$ ([0.00382, 0.00947]). The comparison no longer conflates access to ranking features with the classifier, but separately trained rankers may still differ in their fit. Table~\ref{tab:rankci} isolates ranking effects within each fixed classifier; learned ranking helps all three late-fusion classifiers relative to entropy without $D$, but not uniformly for early fusion. The native AURA learned full ranker improves mean AURC from 0.12598 to 0.12174, a paired difference of $-0.00424$ ([$-0.00568,-0.00280$]).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{revision_figures/risk_coverage.pdf}
\caption{Matched full ranker: macro selective risk, with pointwise 95\% seed-level $t$ bands for selected classifiers. Curves sampled at 0.10--1.00 in 0.05 increments; AURC uses every retained-set size, including exact-tie averaging. Bands are not simultaneous.}
\label{fig:rc}
\end{figure}
\subsection{RQ3: Incremental disagreement and sensitivity}
The primary native ablation holds every class probability and prediction fixed. Adding $4D$ changes AURC from 0.12188 to 0.12174 ($\Delta=-0.000138$, 95\% interval [$-0.000692,0.000415$]); error-detection AUROC changes from 0.7188 to 0.7184. The shared-context AURA ablation likewise yields $-0.000052$ ([$-0.000456,0.000351$]). None of the nine one-factor sensitivity intervals excludes zero (Table~\ref{tab:sens}); their mean effects range from $-0.000313$ to $+0.000029$. The experiments do not establish a useful disagreement gain, nor prove absence of any effect. The early RF shared-context contrast has a small unadjusted negative interval barely excluding zero; it is exploratory and does not establish a general benefit.
'''+T5+r'''The native ranker's mean standardized $4D$ coefficient is 0.01551 (95\% interval [$-0.05819,0.08921$]); five of ten coefficients are positive, range $[-0.09884,0.18703]$. The mean of per-seed odds ratios per standard deviation is 1.02060. Held-out marginal permutation increases AURC by 0.000436 ([$-0.000016,0.000887$]) and decreases error-detection AUROC by 0.000948 ([$-0.000384,0.002279$]). Neither interval excludes zero. These diagnostics are consistent with limited incremental ranking value in this generator. They are not independent evidence of clinical importance; the coefficient and permutation analyses share fitted models and generated data.
\subsection{Stress response and threshold transport}
'''+T6+T7+r'''The combined condition decreases native AURA AUROC from 0.8828 to 0.7422, AUPRC from 0.8341 to 0.4873 and increases ECE from 0.0472 to 0.1439. AUPRC also depends on prevalence, which changes in the Mimic and Combined conditions. At fixed 80\% test coverage, the native full ranker has macro risk 0.18415 versus 0.18114 for AURA entropy: better integrated risk does not guarantee improvement at every operating point. The development-selected score threshold retains 88.36\% of Complete records but only 59.56\% of Combined records, with conditional error 0.1608 and 0.2031, respectively. Table~\ref{tab:stress} and Table~\ref{tab:threshold} deliberately separate these different quantities. The threshold provides neither invariant coverage nor formal error control. The nominal 50\% pre-repair dropout leaves a mean of 1.629 observed modalities versus 2.130 for 30\% dropout. The low-quality combined condition has mean observed quality 0.334 versus 0.715 for complete data. These are implementation audits, not clinical calibration.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{revision_figures/robustness.pdf}
\caption{Mean AUROC across stress conditions, with labels matching the scenario table.}
\label{fig:robust}
\end{figure}
\subsection{Expert performance and attribution stability}
'''+T8+r'''The laboratory expert has the highest mean AUROC on its available records; populations differ across expert availability, so the values are not a controlled modality comparison. Separate expert recalibration does not uniformly improve Brier or ECE under shift. Across all default test conditions, assertions verify normalized weights, zero weights for unavailable modalities and finite predictions. Partition disjointness and exact coalition reconstruction (tolerance $10^{-9}$) are checked, as is first-member logit reconstruction. On the fixed 150-record complete-condition anchor, pairwise mean absolute difference in coalition probability attributions is 0.02143; sign agreement is 97.15\% and largest absolute modality agreement is 90.21\%. For first-member feature-logit attributions, mean absolute differences are 0.04787, 0.05452 and 0.05480 for clinical, laboratory and imaging groups; top-feature agreement is 66.07\%, 91.29\% and 90.10\%, respectively. The anchor experiment measures stability across independent simulator-trained models at fixed inputs, not stability to missingness or clinical explanation fidelity. It does not validate user comprehension, causal effects, clinical usefulness, or the entire ensemble's feature-level explanations.
'''+tex[b:]
a=tex.index('The experiments show why');b=tex.index('One explanation',a)
tex=tex[:a]+r'''The central result is a limit on the methodological claim: a learned ranker improves integrated risk for late fusion, but weighted dispersion contributes no clearly established additional benefit after matching confidence, quality and availability. The sensitivity and permutation checks do not overturn that finding. A simple early-fusion logistic classifier has the best macro discrimination and lowest mean matched AURC, although the shared full-ranker contrast is inconclusive. The gate's small gains against equal and quality weighting are supported by paired intervals, but are not evidence of clinical importance. Algebraic attribution and anchor stability checks characterize model behavior only.

'''+tex[b:]
tex=tex.replace('The early-fusion models were not given a separately trained error ranker; thus their entropy-based comparisons do not isolate ranking quality.','All classifiers receive matched ranking protocols, but the auxiliary experts used for the shared context add computational cost, and this protocol does not exhaust classifier-specific uncertainty methods. Sensitivity analyses are one-factor-at-a-time and reuse the original simulator; they cannot exclude interactions or establish equivalence. The added comparisons were motivated by review and remain exploratory, not prospectively preregistered.')
a=tex.index('AURA provides a reproducible');b=tex.index('\\section*{Data and Code Availability}',a)
tex=tex[:a]+r'''In an axSpA-inspired synthetic benchmark, reliability weighting produces small paired improvements over simple late fusion, and learned error ranking can improve integrated selective risk. The additional weighted disagreement statistic does not show a clearly established benefit under matched ablation or nine sensitivity settings. A simple early logistic model remains a strong control. These results are a negative methodological finding within an illustrative generator, not evidence about real axSpA diagnostic relationships or clinical effectiveness. Independent clinical data, validated quality measures and external evaluation would be necessary for any clinical claim.
'''+tex[b:]
tex=tex.replace('The accompanying source package includes the deterministic generator, experiment script, one generated population, per-seed metrics, audit files, explanation examples, figures and literature matrix.','The accompanying source package includes the deterministic generator, original and revision experiment scripts, table/figure generation script, one generated population, per-seed metrics, sensitivity, permutation and attribution-stability files, audit files and literature matrix.')
tex=tex.replace('The work requires human author verification of references, code, clinical assumptions and conclusions before submission.','GPT-6.2 was the identified model for this revision; the underlying deployment snapshot is not independently verifiable. AI assistance included the text throughout the manuscript, figures, tables and research code, not merely language editing. Human authors retain full responsibility for verifying references, code, clinical assumptions, numerical results and conclusions and for approving the submitted work.')
